@node pfsdoctor "pfsdoctor"
@{b}@{u}pfsdoctor@{uu}@{ub}

pfsdoctor checks and repairs PFS3 volumes ( @{fg shine}PFS\1@{fg text} , @{fg shine}PFS\2@{fg text} ). It works on
the partition of a device, not on a mounted volume. The device is
inhibited while pfsdoctor runs and comes back when it ends.

----------------------------------------
@{"<< Prev" link Main}  @{"Contents" link Main}  @{"Next >>" link Usage}

@endnode

@node Usage "Usage"
@{b}Usage@{ub}

@{fg highlight}
  pfsdoctor DEVICE/A,INFO/S,CHECK/S,REPAIR/S,SEARCH/S,UNFORMAT/S,VERBOSE/S,NONINTERACTIVE/S,LOGFILE/K,DIRECTSCSI/S
@{fg text}

@{fg shine}DEVICE@{fg text} is the device name, with or without the colon, for example @{fg shine}DH0:@{fg text} .
Exactly one of @{fg shine}INFO@{fg text} , @{fg shine}CHECK@{fg text} , @{fg shine}REPAIR@{fg text} , @{fg shine}SEARCH@{fg text} and @{fg shine}UNFORMAT@{fg text} is required.

Example: @{fg shine}pfsdoctor DH0: CHECK@{fg text}

----------------------------------------
@{"<< Prev" link pfsdoctor}  @{"Contents" link Main}  @{"Next >>" link Commands}

@endnode

@node Commands "Commands"
@{b}Commands@{ub}

  @{b}command@{ub}   @{b}what it does@{ub}               @{b}writes@{ub}    
  --------  -------------------------  --------  
  @{fg shine}INFO@{fg text}      shows the rootblock and    no        
            its extension with the               
            options, the reserved                
            area, the free blocks and            
            the PFS version that                 
            formatted the disk                   
  @{fg shine}CHECK@{fg text}     checks the volume once     no        
            and reports each error               
  @{fg shine}REPAIR@{fg text}    checks the volume and      yes       
            fixes what it finds, in              
            passes until a pass finds            
            nothing, at most 7                   
  @{fg shine}SEARCH@{fg text}    has no function of its     possibly  
            own in this version and              
            scans like @{fg shine}CHECK@{fg text} , but               
            without the write                    
            protection of @{fg shine}CHECK@{fg text} , so             
            use @{fg shine}CHECK@{fg text} instead                    
  @{fg shine}UNFORMAT@{fg text}  undoes a quick format and  yes       
            repairs the volume. Back             
            up the data and format               
            the partition afterwards.            

Run @{fg shine}CHECK@{fg text} first and @{fg shine}REPAIR@{fg text} only after a backup of what can still be
read.

----------------------------------------
@{"<< Prev" link Usage}  @{"Contents" link Main}  @{"Next >>" link Options}

@endnode

@node Options "Options"
@{b}Options@{ub}

  @{b}option@{ub}          @{b}effect@{ub}                                 
  --------------  -------------------------------------  
  @{fg shine}VERBOSE@{fg text}         more messages                          
  @{fg shine}NONINTERACTIVE@{fg text}  answers every question with its first  
                  choice (ok, continue), which suits     
                  scripts                                
  @{fg shine}LOGFILE@{fg text}         creates the file, but this version     
                  writes nothing to it, so redirect the  
                  output instead (                       
                  @{fg shine}pfsdoctor DH0: CHECK >RAM:check.log@{fg text} )  
  @{fg shine}DIRECTSCSI@{fg text}      tries direct SCSI access first and     
                  falls back to detecting the access     
                  mode (trackdisk, TD64, NSD, direct     
                  SCSI)                                  

----------------------------------------
@{"<< Prev" link Commands}  @{"Contents" link Main}  @{"Next >>" link Questions}

@endnode

@node Questions "Questions"
@{b}Questions@{ub}

Without @{fg shine}NONINTERACTIVE@{fg text} , pfsdoctor asks before it changes the volume in
a way that cannot be undone. When the rootblock is not found, it offers
to search for a misplaced rootblock and then to build a new one, which
destroys a partition that is not PFS. When it finds a misplaced
partition, it offers to write a mountlist for it. Answer @{fg shine}1@{fg text} for the first
choice and @{fg shine}0@{fg text} to cancel.

----------------------------------------
@{"<< Prev" link Options}  @{"Contents" link Main}  @{"Next >>" link Return_code}

@endnode

@node Return_code "Return code"
@{b}Return code@{ub}

pfsdoctor returns 0 also when it finds or fixes errors, so read its
output.

----------------------------------------
@{"<< Prev" link Questions}  @{"Contents" link Main}

@endnode
